Answer: AUTO_INCREMENT is a column feature, not a constraint. When it is applied to a column, MySQL generates the next numeric value automatically each time a row is inserted, so the value does not have to be typed by hand. It is normally used on ID columns that must be unique and keep increasing (1, 2, 3, ...), such as a primary key. By default the numbering starts from 1. If a different starting number is required, it has to be specified explicitly. A constraint (PRIMARY KEY, NOT NULL, CHECK and so on) is a rule that restricts which values a column will accept. AUTO_INCREMENT does not restrict anything; it supplies the value. Q3. Write a CREATE TABLE statement for a customers table with an auto-incremented primary key, a mandatory first name, a country and a score that must be greater than zero. CREATE TABLE customers ( customer_id INT PRIMARY KEY AUTO_INCREMENT, first_name VARCHAR(20) NOT NULL, country VARCHAR(10), score INT CHECK (score > 0)

